TI  - Effect of soundscapes and crowd density on economic behavior in commercial streets
T2  - APPLIED ACOUSTICS
LA  - English
KW  - Architectural acoustic environment
KW  - Soundscape
KW  - Urban commercial street
KW  - Economic behavior
KW  - Crowd density
KW  - ENVIRONMENT
KW  - RESPONSES
KW  - IMPACT
AB  - Urban commercial streets, as critical nodes of economic activity, exert substantial influence on consumer behavior and commercial performance. However, empirical research investigating how soundscape characteristics and crowd density interact to shape economic behaviors in complex urban open spaces remains limited. This study addresses this gap through a case analysis of a typical commercial district in Yangzhou, China, constructing two integrated indicator systems. From a behavioral economics perspective, five economic behavior metrics are developed: Consumption intention intensity (CII), Affective consumption willingness (ACW), Queuing time (QT), Recommendation to friends (RTF), and Revisit intention (RE). Results reveal that both objective acoustic indicators (e.g., LAeq, L10, L50, L90) and perceptual dimensions of the soundscape (eventfulness, calmness, chaoticness) are significantly associated with these behavioral outcomes. Sound source typologies further moderate behavioral responses via perceived loudness, event frequency, and preference. In terms of spatial social density, the study introduces both planar crowd densities (CPD4, CPD8, CPD12) and fa & ccedil;ade crowd density (CFD) to capture horizontal and vertical perceptions of crowding. Among these, CFD demonstrates strong and consistent correlations with all economic behavior indicators. Based on these findings, the "Sound-Human-Economic (SHE)" model is proposed and validated to conceptualize the interaction mechanisms among acoustic environment, social density, and consumer economic behavior. Sudden sound pressure (SSP), CFD, and Educational level emerge as robust predictors. This research contributes to theoretical advancement in urban acoustic-behavior studies and offers practical implications for evidence-based soundscape design, crowd management, and consumer-centered urban commercial planning.
