AB  - Although extensive evidence suggests that visual exposure to nature can effectively alleviate the emotional distress caused by negative social feedback, it remains unclear how auditory exposure to nature influences emotional responses to such feedback and the underlying processing mechanisms. This study employed electroencephalography to examine the effects of natural sounds on emotional experience and the underlying processing of negative social feedback. Thirty-nine participants completed a social evaluation task while listening to either natural or urban sounds, and subsequently rated their pleasure and arousal levels following the feedback. The behavioral results revealed that the participants reported significantly higher pleasure ratings in response to negative social feedback under natural sound conditions than under urban sound conditions. Event-related potential results demonstrated that natural sounds elicited a significantly larger P2 (a positive deflection around 200ms) amplitude in response to negative feedback relative to urban sounds, reflecting reduced early threat perception and attenuated automatic vigilance. Furthermore, the late positive potential (LPP) amplitude in response to negative feedback was significantly larger, corresponding to more positive evaluative processing at later temporal stages. Correlation analysis showed that both the P2 and LPP amplitudes were positively correlated with subjective pleasure ratings under natural sound conditions. These findings suggest that natural sounds facilitate emotional recovery by modulating the feedback processing across distinct stages.
