TI  - Unveiling the nonlinear effects of street-level audiovisual environments on urban cycling volume
T2  - JOURNAL OF TRANSPORT & HEALTH
LA  - English
KW  - Cycling amount
KW  - Built environment
KW  - Nonlinear effect
KW  - Sustainable transportation
KW  - NOISE
KW  - BICYCLE
KW  - DESIGN
KW  - HEALTH
AB  - Introduction: Built environment attributes are widely recognized as key levers for promoting cycling. However, their implementation is often limited by the spatial and economic constraints of large-scale urban redevelopment. Existing research has largely overlooked the nonlinear and potentially threshold-based relationships between environmental features and cycling behavior. Moreover, cyclists' sensory perceptions of street-level audiovisual environments remain insufficiently considered, despite their importance for environmental comfort and perceived safety. Method: This study integrates interpretable machine learning and computer vision techniques to examine how the perceived audiovisual environment influences cycling volume at the street level. Using large-scale crowdsourced cycling trajectory data from a fitness tracking application and street-level imagery, we derived audiovisual and built-environment indicators to train an XGBoost model. Partial dependence analysis was employed to visualize and interpret the nonlinear effects of key environmental features. Results: The findings reveal that both built and audiovisual factors exhibit complex nonlinear associations with cycling volume, characterized by variable-specific thresholds and saturation effects. Streets with greater visual openness, stronger presence of natural sounds, lower noise intensity, and higher perceived safety were associated with substantially higher cycling volumes. Acoustic attributes, particularly natural and mechanical sound components, showed distinct nonlinear responses. Conclusion: This study provides new empirical evidence that street-level sensory environments significantly influence cycling behavior in nonlinear ways. The results indicate that relatively low-cost design interventions, including enhancing visual openness, using vegetation to reduce noise, and strengthening perceived safety, can support the retrofitting of existing streetscapes. These insights can support planners and policymakers in advancing more inclusive and sustainable urban mobility.
