AB  - Coastal environments are essential habitats for small dolphins but are increasingly affected by urban development, with underwater noise pollution posing significant threats. We investigate spatiotemporal variation in the acoustic behavior of Guiana dolphins in 2 areas of southern Brazil with varying noise levels. Our findings show that the whistling rate decreased by up to 79% during the high-noise period, suggesting reduced social communication or masking effects. When comparing between periods, whistle duration varied only during foraging at the port area, where it decreased by 40 milliseconds. Differences in minimum and delta frequencies of whistles between areas were not linked to noise levels, group size, or environmental conditions-suggesting the influence of other individual or social factors. These findings provide essential baseline data for developing mitigation measures of acoustic impacts on Guiana dolphins and underscore the need for long-term monitoring to better understand the interactions between noise pollution and dolphin behavior.
   Guiana dolphins change how they communicate in noisy waters, with whistling rates dropping by up to 79% during high-noise periods, potentially limiting social communication. Whistle duration shifted depending on the environment, increasing in a quieter area but decreasing near a busy port. Changes in whistle frequency were not directly tied to noise, suggesting other social or individual factors at play. These insights help us understand how human-made noise affects dolphins and highlight the need for better protection of their acoustic environment.
   Ambientes costeiros s & atilde;o habitats essenciais aos pequenos cet & aacute;ceos. Entretanto, s & atilde;o cada vez mais afetados por impactos gerados pelo desenvolvimento urbano, em especial pelas consequ & ecirc;ncias da polui & ccedil;& atilde;o ac & uacute;stica. Nesse trabalho, investigamos a varia & ccedil;& atilde;o espa & ccedil;o-temporal do comportamento ac & uacute;stico de botos-cinza em duas & aacute;reas com diferentes n & iacute;veis de interfer & ecirc;ncia antropog & ecirc;nica, em um complexo estuarino no sul do Brasil. Os resultados indicam a taxa de assobio foi reduzida em at & eacute; 79% durante o per & iacute;odo mais ruidoso, sugerindo redu & ccedil;& atilde;o da comunica & ccedil;& atilde;o social entre os animais, ou efeitos de mascaramento sonoro. Temporalmente, a dura & ccedil;& atilde;o dos assobios variou apenas durante o comportamento de alimenta & ccedil;& atilde;o na & aacute;rea portu & aacute;ria, onde sofreu uma redu & ccedil;& atilde;o de 40 ms. No entanto, as diferen & ccedil;as nos par & acirc;metros ac & uacute;sticos dos assobios entre as & aacute;reas n & atilde;o foram associadas diretamente ao n & iacute;vel de ru & iacute;do, tamanho do grupo ou condi & ccedil;& otilde;es ambientais, sugerindo a influ & ecirc;ncia de outros fatores sociais ou individuais. Esses resultados fornecem dados essenciais para a implementa & ccedil;& atilde;o de medidas de mitiga & ccedil;& atilde;o de impactos ac & uacute;sticos aos botos-cinza, destacando a relev & acirc;ncia do monitoramento a longo prazo para o melhor entendimento das rela & ccedil;& otilde;es entre polui & ccedil;& atilde;o sonora e o comportamento dos cet & aacute;ceos.
