KW  - EMISSIONS
AB  - Urbanization has drastically transformed the acoustic landscape of Indian cities, and Agartala (the capital of Tripura) is increasingly experiencing the adverse effects of noise pollution. This study investigates the impact of vehicular noise pollution at ten major signalized intersections in Agartala, focusing on the relationship between traffic volume, vehicle behavior, and environmental noise levels. Noise measurements were conducted over six months using calibrated Type II sound level meters, covering 16 h per day from 6 AM to 10 PM. The data included observations on traffic delays, engine idling patterns, and the frequency of unnecessary horn use. The results revealed that noise levels ranged between 65.9 dB(A) and 88.8 dB(A) during the day, and 60.3 dB(A) to 82.0 dB(A) at night, exceeding the CPCB-prescribed limits for commercial zones (65 dB and 55 dB, respectively). North Gate and RMS Chowmuhani emerged as the noisiest intersections. Alarmingly, over 99% of drivers were observed idling engines during red lights, and more than 90% engaged in unnecessary horn use, contributing significantly to noise levels. Statistical regression analysis demonstrated strong correlations between noise level, vehicular delay, and traffic volume (R-2 > 0.89). The study emphasizes the urgent need for policy interventions such as enforcement of anti-honking regulations, promotion of High Occupancy Vehicles (HOVs), synchronized traffic signals, and public education campaigns. This research provides a valuable framework for understanding and mitigating traffic-induced noise pollution in small to mid-sized Indian cities and can serve as a model for developing targeted urban noise management strategies.
