KW  - playback experiments
KW  - REPRODUCTIVE SUCCESS
KW  - TEMPERATURE
KW  - BIRD
AB  - Anthropogenic noise pollution disrupts wildlife behavior, physiology, and demography yet species characterized as urban adapters, such as the Eastern Bluebird (Sialia sialis), willingly breed in urban areas. Hypothesizing that noise levels could disrupt incubation behavior, we examined the effect of (a) chronic traffic and (b) episodic construction noises on nest attendance (males, females) and in-nest incubation behaviors (females) in Eastern Bluebird pairs. We used photos taken at nest boxes placed along an anthropogenic noise gradient on the University of Florida campus to identify key behaviors. Nest boxes were in quiet (< 70dB) or traffic noise-exposed areas (> 70dB) and some boxes in each category received additional intermittent playback of construction noises. We used Chit tests, Mahalanobis distances, and confusion matrices to ascertain whether treatments distinctly altered a suite of nest attendance behaviors exhibited by pairs. Playback significantly increased the frequency of peeking and poking from the nest hole by females; males spent more time atop nest boxes in traffic noise-exposed sites; and males spent almost no time on quiet boxes exposed to playback. Findings are consistent with current understanding that anthropogenic noise, especially intermittent loud bursts, disrupts incubation stage behaviors in birds, reducing incubation constancy and, in turn, hatching success.
