TI  - Acoustic differences persist in urban Parus major (Great Tit) over two decades of declining anthropogenic noise in Paris, France
T2  - ORNITHOLOGICAL APPLICATIONS
LA  - English
KW  - anthropogenic effects
KW  - anthropogenic noise
KW  - birdsong
KW  - noise pollution
KW  - Paris
KW  - urban ecology
KW  - effets anthropiques
KW  - bruit anthropique
KW  - chant des oiseaux
KW  - pollution sonore
KW  - & eacute;cologie urbaine
KW  - SONG
KW  - FREQUENCY
KW  - EXPOSURE
AB  - Anthropogenic noise pollution induces changes in the behavior of wild animals. Birds exhibit a well-characterized response to noise pollution: by vocalizing at higher frequencies, birds can escape the masking effects of low-frequency anthropogenic sounds. Although Paris ranks among the world's largest and most densely populated cities, in recent years Parisians have piloted a multi-faceted effort to reduce anthropogenic noise. Are the city's noise reduction efforts sufficient to affect the frequency characteristics of city-dwelling birds? We used directional microphones to collect field recordings of Parus major (Great Tit) in noisy urban sites within Paris and quiet forest sites outside of Paris in Fontainebleau. We recorded birds at both sites during two time periods, separated by two decades, in 2003 and 2023. We collected field measurements of ambient noise using a calibrated sound level meter and we evaluated long-term noise monitoring data collected by the French government. We predicted that birds would sing higher frequency songs in noisy urban Paris compared to quiet natural areas, and we predicted that the magnitude of these differences would diminish in 2023 compared to 2003 given the city's recent noise reduction efforts. We show that urban birds sing at higher frequencies when noise levels increase, matching previous findings from across Europe. Long term data reveal a decline in anthropogenic noise in Paris over the past decades, with average noise levels in 2023 reaching half of their previous intensity. Despite noise reduction efforts, urban birds continue to sing with higher frequencies compared to non-urban birds, and those frequencies have not declined over two decades. We conclude that the noise reduction efforts in Paris have decreased anthropogenic noise levels, but that birds maintain songs with higher minimum frequencies in spite of this change.
   Noise pollution changes the songs of birds: to be heard over the sounds of traffic, birds often sing at higher pitches (higher frequencies). We recorded Parus major (Great Tit) in Paris in 2003 and 2023, over a 20-yr interval when the people of Paris have worked to reduce noise pollution. We ask whether the noise reduction efforts in Paris are enough to change the pitch of the songs of city-dwelling birds? We show that P. major sing at a higher pitch when noise levels are high. We show that Paris has become quieter in recent decades, but even with lower noise levels, the pitch of P. major songs has not changed. Therefore, noise reduction efforts in Paris have decreased noise pollution, but city birds continue to sing at higher pitches than forest birds, even though the city has become quieter.
