AB  - The rapid expansion of cities and transportation networks has led to elevated levels of noise pollution worldwide. The use of vegetative noise - shielding barriers is a sustainable and cost-effective approach that is increasingly needed to address this issue. The present study aims to evaluate the acoustical characteristics of various abundantly available vegetative species categorized as grasses, herbs, shrubs, climbing shrubs, and trees. A normalincidence sound impedance tube was used to measure the Sound Absorption Characteristics (SAC) across the frequency range from 50 Hz to 6600 Hz. The study concludes that different vegetative species exhibit distinct acoustical behaviour. Experimental study was performed on three sample types: 1) leaves (L), with a highest observed Noise Reduction Coefficient (NRC) of 0.85 for the silver oak, 2) stem in combination with leaves, with a highest NRC of 0.77 for madhumalti, and 3) soil substrate and stem in combination with leaves, with a highest NRC of 0.65 for kadi patta. Majorly, species showed mid to higher absorption from 1000 Hz to 6600 Hz frequency range. The correlation parameters revealed, no significant correlation between studied parameters of morphological traits (AL, TL, leaf surface roughness, and MC) - sample parameters (mass, density and surface density) - acoustical parameters (NRC, SAA) of vegetative species. The study also reports the regression analysis (linear and base-10 logarithmically transformed values) for correlating NRC and SAA with leaf thickness, leaf area and density of samples. The paper presents the best sound absorptive vegetative species to use as noise barriers, which are abundantly available in the Indian scenario. This study will be helpful for the design and development of noise-shielding vegetative barriers for controlling environmental noise pollution and in forming policies and landscape planning.
