AB  - In nighttime environments, reduced visual dominance heightens the roles of auditory and olfactory channels, altering landscape perception mechanisms affecting health compared to daytime. However, systematic investigations into the dynamic restorative mechanisms of multisensory perception at night remain limited. This study employed night running as a behavioral context and established a cross-temporal experimental framework to investigate the dynamic effects of multimodal sensory stimuli on physiological and psychological restoration, including their temporal lag effects. Thirty participants underwent a pretest-posttest design with a 20-minute intervention. Physiological and psychological data were synchronously collected and analyzed using correlation analysis and lag models to compare the restorative benefits of different sensory stimuli, including a control group (no additional sensory stimuli), a visual group (Campus, Playground, Greenway, Dwelling District), an auditory group (Birdsong, Chirp, Wind, Water), and an olfactory group (China Rose, Gardenia, Jasmine, Lavender, Osmanthus). Results showed that: (1) auditory stimuli were most beneficial for attention restoration and stress recovery, olfactory stimuli were most effective in enhancing positive well-being, while visual stimuli demonstrated relatively limited overall benefits; (2) Visual-Campus, Auditory-Birdsong, and Olfactory-Gardenia contributed most significantly to restoration outcomes; (3) visual stimuli exhibited a lagged effect on stress recovery, olfactory effects were more immediate, and attention restoration peaked 6-8 min post-intervention across all groups. This study validates the applicability of Attention Restoration Theory and Stress Recovery Theory at night, extends the nocturnal action mechanism of the biophilia hypothesis, and provides new insights and an analytical framework for the design and research of nighttime exercise landscapes.
