AB  - Anthropogenic noise pollution can negatively impact vocal signals and reduce communication among individual birds; however, with the COVID-19 shutdown, ambient noise levels were drastically reduced. While we were investigating how noise influences the relative audibility of vocalizations to female Mountain Chickadees (Poecile gambeli) breeding in nest boxes along an urban/rural interface, the COVID-19 shutdown occurred, creating an opportunity to determine whether cessation of noise altered male behavior and audibility of their vocalizations to females. During the 2019 and 2020 breeding seasons (Apr-Jul), we tracked males during 50 dawn choruses while passively recording from inside nest cavities in Kamloops, British Columbia, Canada. Prior to the COVID-19 shutdown, the relative audibility of songs (but not calls) decreased as males moved farther from the nest. Urban males responded to increasing noise by remaining closer to their nest, likely maximizing audibility to mates, whereas rural males sang farther from the nest, likely maximizing audibility to neighbors. During the COVID-19 shutdown, ambient noise was drastically reduced (mean reduction of 12.40 +/- 1.46 dB from 2019 to 2020), and we detected no differences between habitat types in the distance males sang from the nest or in the relative audibility of signals within the nest box. Our results suggest that anthropogenic noise creates complex dynamics in communication networks, with males facing trade-offs between remaining audible to females within their territory and having their signals transmitted to neighboring birds. However, the birds in our study rapidly altered their behavior with the cessation of noise pollution, suggesting high behavioral flexibility and an ability to rapidly re-acclimate to reduced anthropogenic stressors.
   La pollution sonore anthropique peut avoir un impact n & eacute;gatif sur les signaux vocals et r & eacute;duire la communication entre oiseaux individuels; n & eacute;anmoins, le confinement face au COVID-19 a r & eacute;duit les niveaux de bruit drastiquement. Au moment o & ugrave; nous & eacute;tudions l'influence du bruit sur l'audibilit & eacute; relative des vocalizations des m & eacute;sanges de Gambel (Poecile gambeli) femelles qui nichent dans des bo & icirc;tes & agrave; nid dans une interface urbaine/rurale, le confinement li & eacute; au COVID-19 a eu lieu, ce qui a rendu possible d'& eacute;tudier si l'arr & ecirc;t de bruit changeait le comportement du m & acirc;le et l'audibilit & eacute; de leur vocalizations pour les femelles. Pendant les saisons de reproduction de 2019 et 2020 (d'avril & agrave; juillet), nous avons suivi les m & acirc;les de 50 groupes d'activit & eacute; chorale & agrave; l'aube (& laquo; dawn choruses & raquo;) et nous avons fait de l'& eacute;coute passive dans les cavit & eacute;s de nidification & agrave; Kamloops, British Columbia, Canada. Avant le confinement li & eacute; au COVID-19, l'audibilit & eacute; relative des chants (mais pas des appels) diminuaient quand les m & acirc;les s'& eacute;loignaient du nid. Les m & acirc;les urbains r & eacute;pondaient & agrave; l'augmentation de bruit en restant plus pr & egrave;s du nid, ce qui pouvait maximizer l'audibilit & eacute; de leurs partenaires, tandis que les m & acirc;les ruraux chanteaient plus loin du nid, ce qui pouvait maximizer l'audibilit & eacute; de leurs voisins. Pendant le confinement li & eacute; au COVID-19, le bruit ambiant a diminu & eacute; de mani & egrave;re importante (r & eacute;duction moyenne de12.40 +/- 1.46 dB de 2019 & agrave; 2020), et nous n'avons pas d & eacute;tect & eacute; de diff & eacute;rences ni entre types d'habitats sur la distance du nid & agrave; laquelle les m & acirc;les ont chant & eacute; ni & agrave; l'audibilit & eacute; relative dans les bo & icirc;tes & agrave; nid. Nos r & eacute;sultats sug & egrave;rent que le bruit anthropique produit des dynamiques complexes dans les r & eacute;seaux de communication, dans lesquelles les m & acirc;les doivent envisager un compromis entre rester audibles aux femelles dans leur territoire et transmettre leurs signaux aux oiseaux voisins. N & eacute;anmoins, les oiseaux de notre & eacute;tude ont rapidement chang & eacute; leur comportement quand le bruit ambiant s'est arr & ecirc;t & eacute;, ce qui sug & egrave;re une grande fl & eacute;xibilit & eacute; et une habilet & eacute; de se r & eacute;-acclimater rapidement & agrave; la r & eacute;duction de stresseurs anthropiques.
