AB  - Distributed estimation in the Internet of Things (IoT) often suffers from unreliable communication links and non-Gaussian environmental noise, which significantly degrade the performance of conventional algorithms. Most existing approaches assume ideal channel conditions and Gaussian noise, making them inadequate for realistic IoT scenarios. This article proposes a robust and communication-efficient compressed double-diffusion algorithm based on the maximum correntropy criterion ((CDMCC)-M-2). The proposed method leverages MCC to effectively handle impulsive and non-Gaussian interference. Furthermore, a random projection compression strategy is incorporated to significantly reduce communication overhead while maintaining estimation accuracy, as well as to enhance resilience against wireless link noise. We provide theoretical analysis on convergence and steady-state behavior, and extensive simulations validate the superior robustness and performance of the proposed algorithm over existing MSE-based and uncompressed MCC diffusion methods. The results highlight the practical potential of our approach for resilient and scalable distributed learning in bandwidth-constrained and noise-prone IoT environments.
