KW  - ANOMALIES
KW  - MODEL
AB  - The on-board atomic clock is the core component of the satellite navigation time system, and its frequency stability directly affects the accuracy of navigation and positioning. The random pursuit strategy (RPS) has been shown effectively for behavioral prediction of atomic clocks in practice. In this paper, the optimized random pursuit strategy (ORPS) with stronger robustness to environmental noise and more stable predictive performance than RPS has been presented. OPRS comprises a series of predictors, each trained on a subset of historical data. By introducing Lagrange multipliers, ORPS constructs a minimum uncertainty loss function via correlation matrix and calculates the optimal weights for multiple predictors. A weighted sum of the prediction results from multiple predictors is finally computed. The predicted values obtained by ORPS have the minimum mean square error and uncertainty theoretically. The experimental results show the frequency difference readings of on-board rubidium clocks with respect to the time scale on the ground based on ORPS. Compared to RPS, the improvement achieved by ORPS in root mean square error is modest but statistically significant. On the other hand, the reduction in the estimated standard deviation is significant. Specifically, ORPS reduces the root mean square error by up to 3.76% and the estimated standard deviation by two orders of magnitude. Both theoretical analysis and experimental validation have demonstrated that ORPS is a better choice for serving the on-board atomic clock.
