AB  - Ensuring pedestrian safety in urban environments is a critical challenge, yet conventional warning systems that rely on strong multimodal stimuli can often be intrusive or disrupt the user experience. To address this, we propose an auditory interface that augments approaching vehicle sounds using dynamic frequency shifting as time to collision (TTC) decreases. Rather than adding explicit alarm tones, the method aims to embed salience in the existing soundscape while preserving ecological realism. We conducted a VR-based listening study (N=30) using recorded urban ambience and vehicle sounds, manipulating the frequency shift ratio ( 0.5x , 1 x, 2 x, 3x ) and vehicle speed (30 vs. 40 km/h). Results showed that stronger upward frequency shifting led participants to (i) predict collisions earlier, (ii) overestimate vehicle speed, and (iii) judge the vehicle as closer than it physically was. Importantly, upward frequency shifting up to 3 x did not significantly reduce perceived realism compared with unmodified sounds in most conditions. These findings indicate that dynamic frequency shifting can systematically bias TTC, speed, and distance judgments in a conservative direction and may therefore support unobtrusive pedestrian attention guidance. Long-term acceptance, habituation, and effects on real-world behavior require further investigation.
